    "https://emploi.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/job-offers/chercheur-post-doctoral-greenland-runoff-monitoring-from-passive-seismology-f-h--1829598.kjsp?RH=1681202200872": {
      "title": "Chercheur (post-doctoral) greenland runoff monitoring from passive seismology (f/h)",
      "description": "Chercheur (post-doctoral) greenland runoff monitoring from passive seismology (f/h) - Emploi - Université Grenoble Alpes Université Grenoble Alpes Practical informations Contacts Campuses map English FR - Français EN - English Job site Welcome Search Open the main menu Open the search engine EN Working at UGA UGA in numbers Recruitment process Careers Senior Lecturers and Professors Fixed-term Contract Faculty Members Temporary Teaching and Research Assistants (ATER) Lecturers and Lecturer-researchers Lecteurs and Maîtres de Langues (Native Speaker Assistants) PhD Students and Researchers Job Offers Competitive Exams Practical informations Contacts Campuses map Université Grenoble Alpes Close the mobile menu FR - Français EN - English Working at UGA Careers Job Offers Competitive Exams LinkedIn Instagram You are here: Home Job Offers Chercheur (post-doctoral) greenland runoff monitoring from passive seismology (f/h) Response deadline: October 18, 2026 Apply Recrutement type: Open position: Temporary contract Workload: 100% Duration of the contract: From November 01, 2026 to September 30, 2027 Location: IGE OSUG B 460 Rue de la piscine 38400 Saint Martin d'Hères About the organization The Institute of Environmental Geosciences (IGE) is a public research laboratory under the joint supervision of the CNRS/INSU, IRD, Université Grenoble Alpes (UGA), and Grenoble INP. It brings together approximately 240 people, including 145 permanent staff members (researchers, faculty members, and engineers) and around 95 PhD students, postdoctoral researchers, and fixed-term contract staff. The laboratory also hosts several dozen interns and visiting scientists each year. The laboratory is located across two sites on the Grenoble university campus (MCP, OSUG-B, and Glacio sites). IGE is one of the main laboratories of the Grenoble Observatory of Earth and Universe Sciences (OSUG), a federative structure of the INSU. You will be supervised by Florent Gimbert, a CNRS Research Scientist within the CryoDyn team. The overall objective of the CryoDyn team is to better understand and describe the processes that control ice dynamics, in order to better characterize and predict the evolution of glaciers, ice sheets, and sea ice. Your mission As part of the five-year Aria GRuMPS project, you will contribute to improving our understanding of the melting of the Greenland Ice Sheet and its impact on the climate. Your objective: to develop an innovative method based on passive seismology to measure meltwater discharge, both beneath and downstream of glaciers. Measurements carried out on land-terminating glaciers will then be used to estimate the discharge of marine-terminating glaciers, which are difficult to observe directly. A research project at the intersection of seismology, glaciology, and climate modelling, addressing key challenges related to current climate change. Main activities Deploy seismological equipment in the field, in collaboration with the University of Sheffield and the Asiaq Institute in Greenland Analyse and combine seismological data with different observations: field-measured discharge, modelled surface melt, and subglacial water pressure Interpret observations and model the physical processes associated with flow turbulence and sediment transport Extrapolate the results to marine-terminating glaciers, where direct observations are more difficult to obtain Put the results into perspective to improve our understanding of the Greenland Ice Sheet’s response to climate change Preferred qualifications Skills in seismology and fluid mechanics Ability to work in the field, particularly during equipment installation campaigns Good command of English, both written and spoken Strong interest in teamwork and international collaborations Interest in glaciology and climate change-related issues Experience or ease working in mountainous environments would be an asset Desired qualification: PhD in geophysics, mechanics, or mechanical physics Diplomas required PhD ou Doctorate Salary Starting at €3020 gross per month, depending on experience. Apply Published on  October 5, 2026 Updated on  October 5, 2026 For any futher information - About the recruitment process Mathilde BALAUD, recruitment officer mathilde.balaud@univ-grenoble-alpes.fr - About the position Sana Askri - Gestionnaire RH - sana.askri@univ-grenoble-alpes.fr Florent Gimbert - Chercheur - florent.gimbert@univ-grenoble-alpes.fr Why join us Have a meaningfull job Benefit from the attractiveness of the area. Benefit from social adventages. Achieve a good work-life balance. Be accompanied and supported in your career. Work on an inspirative and dynamic. Read more UGA in numbers 57 000 students 10 800 employees 30 academic divisions and units 71 research units, 13 Labex and 7 Equipex www.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr The « HR exellence in research » label The University obtain the HR exellence in research label in April 2021 2021 which was awarded by the European Commission. Read more Print Share Share this on Facebook Share this on LinkedIn Job Offers Université Grenoble Alpes Direction générale déléguée aux Ressources humaines 1180 avenue Centrale 38400 Saint-Martin-d'Hères France +33 (0)4 57 42 21 42 Legal notices Personal data Credits Data protection policy Cookies Accessibility: not compliant Sitemap Follow us LinkedIn Instagram URL of the page https://emploi.univ-grenoble-alpes.fr/job-offers/chercheur-post-doctoral-greenland-runoff-monitoring-from-passive-seismology-f-h--1829598.kjsp?RH=1681202200872 Page title Share the link Copy Copied Close the modal window Share the URL of this page I recommend: Consultable at this address: La page sera alors accessible depuis votre menu \"Mes favoris\". Stop video Play video Mute Play audio Chat: A question? Chatbot Robo Fabrica Matomo traffic statistics X (formerly Twitter) What are you looking for ? Enter a keyword and press Validate Search on text : More"
